# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Define the projected quantum kernel {#define-the-projected-quantum-kernel}"
msgstr "Tukuyin ang projected quantum kernel {#define-the-projected-quantum-kernel}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The projected quantum kernel is defined with the following kernel function: $$k^{\\textrm{PQ}}(x_i, x_j) = \\textrm{exp} \\Big(-\\gamma \\sum_k \\sum_{P \\in \\{ X,Y,Z \\}} (\\textrm{Tr}[P \\rho_k(x_i)] - \\textrm{Tr}[P \\rho_k(x_j)])^2 \\Big) $$\n"
"In the above equation, $\\gamma>0$ is a tunable hyperparameter. The $K^{\\textrm{PQ}}_{ij} = k^{\\textrm{PQ}}(x_i, x_j)$ are the entries of the kernel matrix $K^{\\textrm{PQ}}$.\n"
"Using the definition of 1-RDMs, we can see that the individual terms within the kernel function can be evaluated as $\\textrm{Tr}[P \\rho_k (x_i)] = \\braket P$, where $P \\in \\{ X,Y,Z \\}$. These expectation values are precisely what we measured above.\n"
"By using ``scikit-learn``, we can in fact compute the kernel even more easily. This is due to the readily available radial basis function (``'rbf'``) kernel: $ \\textrm{exp} (-\\gamma \\lVert x - x' \\rVert^2)$. First, we simply need to reshape the new projected training and test datasets into two-dimensional arrays.\n"
"Note that going over the entire dataset can take about 80 minutes on the QPU. To make sure that the rest of the tutorial is easily executable, we additionally provide projections from a previously run experiment (which are included in the files you downloaded in the `Download dataset` code block). If you performed the training yourself, you can continue the tutorial with your own results.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang projected quantum kernel ay tinukoy gamit ang sumusunod na kernel function: $$k^{\\textrm{PQ}}(x_i, x_j) = \\textrm{exp} \\Big(-\\gamma \\sum_k \\sum_{P \\in \\{ X,Y,Z \\}} (\\textrm{Tr}[P \\rho_k(x_i)] - \\textrm{Tr}[P \\rho_k(x_j)])^2 \\Big) $$\n"
"Sa equation sa itaas, ang $\\gamma>0$ ay isang naaayos na hyperparameter. Ang $K^{\\textrm{PQ}}_{ij} = k^{\\textrm{PQ}}(x_i, x_j)$ ay mga entry ng kernel matrix na $K^{\\textrm{PQ}}$.\n"
"Gamit ang kahulugan ng 1-RDMs, makikita natin na ang bawat term sa loob ng kernel function ay maaaring suriin bilang $\\textrm{Tr}[P \\rho_k (x_i)] = \\braket P$, kung saan ang $P \\in \\{ X,Y,Z \\}$. Ang mga expectation value na ito ay eksakto ang mga nasukat natin sa itaas.\n"
"Sa pamamagitan ng ``scikit-learn``, maaari nating kalkulahin ang kernel nang mas madali pa. Ito ay dahil sa madaling magamit na radial basis function (``'rbf'``) kernel: $ \\textrm{exp} (-\\gamma \\lVert x - x' \\rVert^2)$. Una, kailangan lamang nating baguhin ang hugis ng bagong projected na mga training at test dataset sa dalawang dimensyonal na array.\n"
"Tandaan na ang pagdaan sa buong dataset ay maaaring tumagal ng humigit-kumulang 80 minuto sa QPU. Upang masiguro na ang natitirang bahagi ng tutorial ay madaling maipatupad, nagbibigay din kami ng mga projection mula sa isang nakaraang eksperimento (na kasama sa mga file na na-download ninyo sa `Download dataset` code block). Kung ikaw ay nagsagawa ng pagsasanay sa iyong sarili, maaari kang magpatuloy sa tutorial gamit ang iyong sariling mga resulta.\n"

